    {
      "type": "tldr",
      "visible": true,
      "title": "TL;DR",
      "content": {
        "text": "\n**GAM** makes a pretrained Geometric Foundation Model the shared substrate for action decoding and future geometry prediction. The headline story is speed plus CAMERA robustness: **6.9 ms inference** and 83.1% success under LIBERO-Plus camera perturbations.",
        "blockHeight": 148,
        "blockAlign": "center",
        "blockWidth": 1088
      },
      "id": "tldr-section",
      "contentMaxWidth": 1056,
      "paddingTop": 6,
      "paddingBottom": 39
    },
    {
      "type": "abstract",
      "visible": true,
      "title": "Abstract",
      "content": {
        "text": "Generalist robot policies must follow user instructions while reasoning about how objects, cameras, and robot actions interact in the 3D physical world. Recent vision-language-action models and video world-action models inherit strong semantic or temporal priors from large-scale foundation models, but they still operate primarily on 2D image frames or 2D-derived latent spaces, leaving the geometry required for contact-rich manipulation implicit.\n\nWe propose Geometric Action Model (GAM), a language-conditioned manipulation policy that directly repurposes a pretrained Geometric Foundation Model (GFM) as a shared substrate for perception, temporal prediction, and action decoding. GAM splits the GFM at an intermediate layer, where the shallow layers serve as an observation encoder, while a causal future predictor is inserted at the split layer to forecast future latent tokens conditioned on language, proprioception, and action history. The predicted future tokens are then routed through the remaining GFM blocks during feature propagation and decoding, allowing a single backbone to produce both future geometry and actions.\n\nThis design equips the GFM with language-conditioned temporal world modeling using only minimal architectural modification, while preserving its rich geometric priors. On a broad suite of simulation and real-robot manipulation benchmarks, GAM is more accurate, more robust, faster, and lighter than current foundation-model-scale baselines."
      },
      "id": "abstract-section",
      "paddingTop": 12
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "title": "Methodology",
      "content": {
        "text": "GAM splits the pretrained GFM at layer $L_s$ and inserts a causal future predictor between shallow observation encoding and deep geometric decoding.\n\nThe predictor receives language, proprioception, previous action, and geometric tokens, then forecasts both future latent geometry and action tokens. The remaining GFM blocks propagate those predicted tokens, letting the final action head benefit from the same 3D decoding pathway used for future depth.\n\nThe training objective couples action execution with world modeling:\n\n$$L_{total}=\\lambda_{act}L_{act}+\\lambda_{feat}L_{feat}+\\lambda_{depth}L_{depth}$$\n\n### Ablation Study: Split Layer Control\nThe split layer $L_s$ determines whether predicted tokens can become usable 3D structure. The best split is $L_s=12$, where shallow GFM layers have extracted enough visual structure and deep GFM layers still have enough capacity to decode future geometry.\n\nWhen the predictor is inserted too early, the tokens are under-formed. When it is inserted too late, the remaining GFM blocks cannot integrate predicted future tokens into the pretrained geometric prior.",
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "",
            "caption": "Figure 2: Observation encoding, future prediction, and action decoding inside one GFM.",
            "alt": "GAM architecture diagram"
          }
        ]
      },
      "id": "method-section"
    },
